From: HP-UX 11.23 port notes
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] #undef REVISION, which HP-UX <langinfo.h> defines as an integer

HP-UX 11.23 <langinfo.h> has "#define REVISION 64" (an obsolete nl_langinfo
item code). nano uses "#ifdef REVISION" to mean "this is a git build and
REVISION is the commit string", so the clash makes version() execute
printf("%s", 64) and segfault on the very first --version. It compiles and
links silently; only -Wformat hints at it.

A release tarball defines no REVISION of its own, so dropping the system one
restores the intended release branch.
--- a/src/nano.c
+++ b/src/nano.c
@@ -31,6 +31,18 @@
 #endif
 #ifdef ENABLE_UTF8
 #include <langinfo.h>
+
+/* HP-UX 11.23's <langinfo.h> defines REVISION as an OBSOLETE nl_langinfo item code -- the
+ * integer 64.  nano uses "#ifdef REVISION" to mean "this is a git build and REVISION is the
+ * commit string", so the clash makes version() do printf("%s", 64) and segfault on the very
+ * first --version.  It compiles and links silently; only -Wformat hints at it.  A release
+ * tarball defines no REVISION of its own, so dropping the system one restores the intended
+ * release branch.  (Building from git on HP-UX would lose the commit string in --version --
+ * cosmetic, and preferable to a crash.)
+ */
+#if defined(__hpux) && defined(REVISION)
+#undef REVISION
+#endif
 #endif
 #include <locale.h>
 #include <string.h>
